1
00:00:07,351 --> 00:00:10,253
frightful nightmare, and seeing before me a

2
00:00:10,253 --> 00:00:13,971
terrible red glare, crossed with thick black bars.

3
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:20,510
I heard voices too, speaking with a hollow sound, and as if muffled by a rush of wind

4
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:24,222
or water, agitation, uncertainty, and an

5
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:29,010
all-predominating sense of terror confused my faculties.

6
00:00:30,375 --> 00:00:36,730
Air long, I became aware that someone was handling me, lifting me up and supporting

7
00:00:36,731 --> 00:00:39,818
me in a sitting posture, and that more tenderly

8
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,831
than I had ever been raised or upheld before.

9
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:48,070
I rested my head against a pillow or an arm, and felt easy.

10
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:52,670
In five minutes more, the cloud of bewilderment dissolved.

11
00:00:53,619 --> 00:00:56,456
I knew quite well that I was in my own bed,

12
00:00:56,458 --> 00:00:59,630
and that the red glare was the nursery fire.

13
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:02,909
It was night, a candle burnt on the table.

14
00:01:03,849 --> 00:01:07,471
Bessie stood at the bed-foot with a basin in her hand, and

15
00:01:07,472 --> 00:01:11,550
a gentleman sat in a chair near my pillow, leaning over me.

16
00:01:12,139 --> 00:01:17,650
I felt an inexpressible relief, a soothing conviction of protection and

17
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:23,809
security, when I knew that there was a stranger in the room, an individual not

18
00:01:23,811 --> 00:01:27,449
belonging to Gateshead and not related to Mrs. Reed.

19
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:33,840
Turning from Bessie, though her presence was far less obnoxious

20
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,690
to me than that of Abbott, for instance, would have been.

21
00:01:37,730 --> 00:01:39,889
I scrutinized the face of the gentleman.

22
00:01:40,869 --> 00:01:41,869
I knew him.

23
00:01:42,449 --> 00:01:47,789
It was Mr. Lloyd, an apothecary, sometimes called in by Mrs. Reed when the

24
00:01:47,791 --> 00:01:51,969
servants were ailing, for herself and the children she employed her physician.

25
00:01:53,485 --> 00:01:55,190
Well, who am I?

26
00:01:55,869 --> 00:01:56,869
He asked.

27
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:01,030
I pronounced his name, offering him at the same time, my hand.

28
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:06,030
He took it, smiling and saying, we shall do very well by and by.

29
00:02:07,155 --> 00:02:11,800
Then he laid me down, and addressing Bessie, charged her to

30
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:14,670
be very careful that I was not disturbed during the night.

31
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:19,108
Having given some further directions and intimated that he

32
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:23,429
should call again the next day, he departed to my grief.

33
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:28,489
I felt so sheltered and befriended while he sat in the chair near my pillow,

34
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:35,630
and as he closed the door after him, all the room darkened and my heart again sank.

35
00:02:36,670 --> 00:02:38,730
Inexpressible sadness weighed it down.

36
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:42,929
Do you feel as if you should sleep, miss?

37
00:02:43,719 --> 00:02:47,664
Asked Bessie, rather softly, scarcely dared I answer

38
00:02:47,664 --> 00:02:50,510
her, for I feared the next sentence might be rough.

39
00:02:51,329 --> 00:02:52,329
I will try.

40
00:02:53,295 --> 00:02:55,710
Would you like to drink, or could you eat anything?

41
00:02:56,610 --> 00:02:58,409
No, thank you, Bessie.

42
00:02:59,264 --> 00:03:03,110
Then I think I shall go to bed, for it is past 12 o'clock.

43
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:06,489
But you may call me if you want anything in the night.

44
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:09,230
Wonderful civility this.

45
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:11,889
It emboldened me to ask a question.

46
00:03:12,489 --> 00:03:14,889
Bessie what is the matter with me, am I ill?

47
00:03:15,690 --> 00:03:18,349
You fell sick, I suppose, in the red room with crying.

48
00:03:19,389 --> 00:03:21,269
You'll be better soon, no doubt.

49
00:03:22,909 --> 00:03:25,969
Bessie went into the Housemaid's apartment, which was near.

50
00:03:27,309 --> 00:03:32,070
I heard her say, Sarah, come and sleep with me in the nursery.

51
00:03:33,289 --> 00:03:36,409
I daren't for my life be alone with that poor child tonight.

52
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:38,230
She might die.

53
00:03:38,809 --> 00:03:41,150
It's such a strange thing she should have that fit.

54
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:43,489
I wonder if she saw anything.

55
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:45,769
Mrs. was rather too hard.

56
00:03:47,949 --> 00:03:49,250
Sarah came back with her.

57
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:51,010
They both went to bed.

58
00:03:51,670 --> 00:03:54,789
They were whispering together for half an hour before they fell asleep.

59
00:03:55,969 --> 00:03:59,960
I caught scraps of their conversation, from which I was able

60
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:04,170
only too distinctly to infer the main subject discussed.

61
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:09,710
Something passed her, all dressed in white and vanished.

62
00:04:10,539 --> 00:04:14,329
A great black dog behind him, three loud raps on the chamber door.

63
00:04:15,409 --> 00:04:18,550
A light in the churchyard just over his grave, etc., etc.

64
00:04:20,054 --> 00:04:21,730
At last, both slept.

65
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:24,449
The fire and the candle went out.

66
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:30,170
For me, the watches of that long night passed in ghastly wakefulness.

67
00:04:30,949 --> 00:04:34,010
Ear, eye and mind were alike strained by dread.

68
00:04:34,829 --> 00:04:37,309
Such dread as children only can feel.

69
00:04:38,670 --> 00:04:43,429
No severe or prolonged bodily illness followed this incident of the red room.

70
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:48,849
It only gave my nerves a shock of which I feel the reverberation to this day.

71
00:04:49,889 --> 00:04:55,769
Yes, Mrs. Reed, to you I owe some fearful pangs of mental suffering.

72
00:04:56,789 --> 00:05:00,429
But I ought to forgive you, for you knew not what you did.

73
00:05:01,579 --> 00:05:04,097
While rending my heartstrings, you thought

74
00:05:04,097 --> 00:05:07,010
you were only uprooting my bad propensities.

75
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:11,170
Next day, by noon, I was up and dressed and

76
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:15,210
sat wrapped in a shawl by the nursery hearth.

77
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:17,750
I felt physically weak and broken down.

78
00:05:18,929 --> 00:05:23,389
But my worse ailment was an unutterable wretchedness of mind.

79
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:30,110
A wretchedness which kept drawing from me silent tears, no sooner had I wiped one

80
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,829
salt drop from my cheek than another followed.

81
00:05:33,969 --> 00:05:36,889
Yet, I thought, I ought to have been happy.

82
00:05:37,909 --> 00:05:39,989
For none of the reeds were there, they were

83
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:42,431
all gone out in the carriage with their mamma.

84
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:49,010
Abbott, too, was sowing in another room, and Bessie, as she moved hither and

85
00:05:49,010 --> 00:05:53,050
thither, putting away toys and arranging drawers, addressed

86
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:57,449
to me every now and then a word of unwanted kindness.

87
00:05:59,585 --> 00:06:05,010
This state of things should have been to me a paradise of peace, accustomed as I

88
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:08,789
was to a life of ceaseless reprimand and thankless fagging.

89
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:15,489
But in fact, my racked nerves were now in such a state that no calm could soothe,

90
00:06:16,429 --> 00:06:18,630
and no pleasure excite them agreeably.

91
00:06:20,429 --> 00:06:26,710
Bessie had been down into the kitchen, and she brought up with her a tart on a

92
00:06:26,710 --> 00:06:32,849
certain brightly painted china plate, whose bird of paradise, nestling in a

93
00:06:32,851 --> 00:06:36,561
wreath of convolvuli and rose buds, had been won't to

94
00:06:36,563 --> 00:06:40,670
stir in me a most enthusiastic sense of admiration.

95
00:06:42,079 --> 00:06:45,610
And which plate I had often petitioned to be allowed to take in my hand,

96
00:06:46,210 --> 00:06:47,989
in order to examine it more closely.

97
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:52,849
But had always hitherto been deemed unworthy of such a privilege.

98
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:57,925
This precious vessel was now placed on my knee, and I was

99
00:06:57,927 --> 00:07:01,889
cordially invited to eat the circulate of delicate pastry upon it.

100
00:07:02,809 --> 00:07:09,809
Vane favor coming, like most other favors long deferred and often wished for,

101
00:07:10,489 --> 00:07:11,489
too late.

102
00:07:12,389 --> 00:07:17,070
I could not eat the tart, and the plumage of the bird, the tints of the flowers,

103
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:19,570
seemed strangely faded.

104
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:22,789
I put both plate and tart away.

105
00:07:24,230 --> 00:07:25,829
Bessie asked if I would have a book.

106
00:07:26,809 --> 00:07:30,974
The word book acted as a transient stimulus, and I

107
00:07:30,974 --> 00:07:34,349
begged her to fetch gulliver's travels from the library.

108
00:07:34,909 --> 00:07:38,590
This book I had again and again perused with delight.

109
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:43,920
I considered it a narrative of facts, and discovered in it

110
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:47,809
a vein of interest deeper than what I found in fairy tales.

111
00:07:49,250 --> 00:07:53,853
For as to the elves, having sought them in vain among fox-clove

112
00:07:53,853 --> 00:07:57,769
leaves and bells under mushrooms and beneath the ground.

113
00:07:59,739 --> 00:08:05,309
Ivy mantling old walnuts, I had at length made up my mind to the sad truth,

114
00:08:06,050 --> 00:08:11,409
that they were all gone out of England, to some savage country, where the woods

115
00:08:11,411 --> 00:08:14,909
were wilder and thicker, and the population more scant.

116
00:08:16,070 --> 00:08:22,070
Whereas Lilliput and Rob Dignag being in my creed, solid parts of the earth's

117
00:08:22,070 --> 00:08:29,010
surface, I doubted not that I might one day, by taking a long voyage, see with my

118
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:33,735
own eyes the little fields, houses and trees, the diminutive

119
00:08:33,737 --> 00:08:38,690
people, the tiny cows, sheep and birds of the one realm.

120
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:44,289
And the cornfields forest high, the mighty mastiffs, the monster cats,

121
00:08:45,110 --> 00:08:47,610
the tower-like men and women of the other.

122
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:54,130
Yet, when this cherished volume was now placed in my hand, when I turned over its

123
00:08:54,130 --> 00:08:59,190
leaves and sought in its marvelous pictures the charm I had, till now,

124
00:08:59,750 --> 00:09:00,769
never failed to find.

125
00:09:01,619 --> 00:09:08,269
All was eerie and dreary, the giants were gaunt goblins, the pigmies, malevolent and

126
00:09:08,271 --> 00:09:11,074
fearful imps, gulliver and most desolate

127
00:09:11,076 --> 00:09:14,891
wanderer in most dread and dangerous regions.

128
00:09:16,409 --> 00:09:21,349
I closed the book, which I dared no longer peruse and put it on the table,

129
00:09:21,750 --> 00:09:23,349
beside the untasted tart.

130
00:09:24,909 --> 00:09:29,130
Bessie had now finished dusting and tidying the room, and having washed her

131
00:09:29,130 --> 00:09:34,989
hands, she opened a certain little drawer full of splendid shreds of silk and satin,

132
00:09:35,670 --> 00:09:38,429
and began making a new bonnet for Georgiana's doll.

133
00:09:39,309 --> 00:09:45,269
Meantime, she sang, her song was, in the days when we went gypsying,

134
00:09:45,969 --> 00:09:46,969
a long time ago.

135
00:09:47,599 --> 00:09:51,610
I had often heard the song before, and always with lively delight.

136
00:09:52,230 --> 00:09:55,449
For Bessie had a sweet voice, at least I thought so.

137
00:09:56,250 --> 00:09:59,487
But now, though her voice was still sweet, I

138
00:09:59,487 --> 00:10:03,391
found in its melody an indescribable sadness.

139
00:10:03,659 --> 00:10:07,889
Sometimes preoccupied with her work, she sang the refrain very low,

140
00:10:08,130 --> 00:10:09,130
very lingeringly.

141
00:10:10,175 --> 00:10:14,630
A long time ago came out like the saddest cadence of a funeral hymn.

142
00:10:15,809 --> 00:10:19,650
She passed into another ballad, and this time a really dullful one.

143
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:24,117
My feet they are sore and my limbs they are weary,

144
00:10:24,119 --> 00:10:26,550
long as the way, and the mountains are wild.

145
00:10:27,130 --> 00:10:29,476
Soon will the twilight close moonless and

146
00:10:29,478 --> 00:10:33,210
dreary over the path of the poor orphan child.

147
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,427
Why, did they send me so far and so lonely, up

148
00:10:36,429 --> 00:10:39,389
where the more spread and grey rocks are piled?

149
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:43,530
Men are hard-hearted and kind angels only.

150
00:10:44,244 --> 00:10:46,550
Watch over the steps of a poor orphan child.

151
00:10:47,329 --> 00:10:52,809
Yet distant and soft, the night breeze is blowing, clouds there are none,

152
00:10:53,639 --> 00:10:55,429
and clear stars be mild.

153
00:10:56,409 --> 00:11:01,929
God in his mercy, protection is showing comfort and hope to the poor orphan child.

154
00:11:02,949 --> 00:11:06,282
Evan should I fall or the broken bridge passing,

155
00:11:06,284 --> 00:11:09,451
or stray in the marshes by false lights beguiled.

156
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:13,248
Still will my father with promise and blessing

157
00:11:13,248 --> 00:11:16,670
take to his bosom the poor orphan child.

158
00:11:17,639 --> 00:11:20,211
There is a thought that for strength should avail

159
00:11:20,213 --> 00:11:23,409
me, though both of shelter and kindred dispoiled.

160
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:27,489
Evan is a home, and arrest will not fail me.

161
00:11:28,269 --> 00:11:30,909
God is a friend to the poor orphan child.

162
00:11:32,309 --> 00:11:36,650
Come, Miss Jane, don't cry, said Bessie, as she finished.

163
00:11:37,630 --> 00:11:41,652
She might as well have said to the fire, don't burn, but how

164
00:11:41,652 --> 00:11:45,289
could she divine the morbid suffering to which I was a prey?

165
00:11:46,905 --> 00:11:49,409
In the course of the morning, Mr. Lloyd came again.

166
00:11:51,119 --> 00:11:57,190
What, already up, said he, as he entered the nursery, wellness, how is she?

167
00:11:58,809 --> 00:12:00,889
Bessie answered that I was doing very well.

168
00:12:02,219 --> 00:12:03,750
Then she ought to look more cheerful.

169
00:12:04,650 --> 00:12:05,650
Come here, Miss Jane.

170
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,050
Your name is Jane, is it not?

171
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:11,489
Yes, sir, Jane air.

172
00:12:12,980 --> 00:12:15,949
Well, you have been crying, Miss Jane air.

173
00:12:16,849 --> 00:12:17,929
Can you tell me what about?

174
00:12:18,309 --> 00:12:19,309
Have you any pain?

175
00:12:20,210 --> 00:12:21,210
No, sir.

176
00:12:22,179 --> 00:12:25,052
Oh, I dare say she's crying because she could not go out with Mrs.

177
00:12:25,054 --> 00:12:26,411
in the carriage.

178
00:12:27,269 --> 00:12:28,269
Interposed, Bessie?

179
00:12:29,789 --> 00:12:30,789
Surely not.

180
00:12:31,349 --> 00:12:33,769
Why, she is too old for such petitioness.

181
00:12:35,269 --> 00:12:38,077
I thought so too, and myself a steam being

182
00:12:38,077 --> 00:12:41,951
wounded by the false charge, I answered promptly.

183
00:12:42,570 --> 00:12:44,789
I never cried for such a thing in my life.

184
00:12:45,630 --> 00:12:47,250
I hate going out in the carriage.

185
00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:49,809
I cry because I am miserable.

186
00:12:51,300 --> 00:12:53,449
Oh, fee miss, said Bessie.

187
00:12:55,090 --> 00:12:57,329
The good apothecary appeared a little puzzled.

188
00:12:58,469 --> 00:13:00,309
I was standing before him.

189
00:13:01,389 --> 00:13:03,789
He fixed his eyes on me very steadily.

190
00:13:04,789 --> 00:13:08,259
His eyes were small and gray, not very bright,

191
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,911
but I dare say I should think them shrewd now.

192
00:13:12,730 --> 00:13:15,809
He had a hard featured yet good-natured looking face.

193
00:13:16,690 --> 00:13:21,489
Having considered me at leisure, he said, what made you ill yesterday?

194
00:13:22,659 --> 00:13:26,309
She had a fall, said Bessie, again putting in her word.

195
00:13:27,050 --> 00:13:28,050
Fall?

196
00:13:28,739 --> 00:13:30,349
Why, that is like a baby again.

197
00:13:31,230 --> 00:13:32,909
Can't she manage to walk at her age?

198
00:13:33,289 --> 00:13:35,010
She must be eight or nine years old.

199
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:37,869
I was knocked down.

200
00:13:38,570 --> 00:13:43,789
Was the blunt explanation jerked out of me by another pang of mortified pride.

201
00:13:44,699 --> 00:13:47,851
But that did not make me ill, I added, while

202
00:13:47,853 --> 00:13:51,570
Mr. Lloyd helped himself to a pinch of snuff.

203
00:13:52,719 --> 00:13:55,822
As he was returning the box to his waistcoat

204
00:13:55,822 --> 00:13:58,971
pocket, a loud bell rang for the servant's dinner.

205
00:13:59,590 --> 00:14:00,670
He knew what it was.

206
00:14:02,090 --> 00:14:04,449
That's for you, nurse, said he.

207
00:14:05,190 --> 00:14:06,190
You can go down.

208
00:14:06,829 --> 00:14:09,389
I'll give Miss Jane a lecture till you come back.

209
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:15,190
Bessie would rather have stayed, but she was obliged to go, because

210
00:14:15,190 --> 00:14:19,110
punctuality at meals was rigidly enforced at Gateshead Hall.

211
00:14:20,159 --> 00:14:21,989
The fall did not make you ill.

212
00:14:22,650 --> 00:14:23,650
What did then?

213
00:14:24,369 --> 00:14:26,929
Pursued Mr. Lloyd when Bessie was gone.

214
00:14:28,539 --> 00:14:32,130
I was shut up in a room where there is a ghost till after dark.

215
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:36,949
I saw Mr. Lloyd smile and frown at the same time.

216
00:14:37,750 --> 00:14:38,090
Ghost?

217
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:38,949
What?

218
00:14:39,210 --> 00:14:40,489
You were a baby after all.

219
00:14:40,949 --> 00:14:41,989
You were afraid of ghosts?

220
00:14:42,949 --> 00:14:44,909
Of Mr. Reed's ghost I am.

221
00:14:45,670 --> 00:14:48,210
He died in that room and was laid out there.

222
00:14:49,449 --> 00:14:53,449
Neither Bessie nor anyone else will go into it at night if they can help it.

223
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:57,050
And it was cruel to shut me up alone without a candle.

224
00:14:57,900 --> 00:15:01,349
So cruel that I think I shall never forget it.

225
00:15:02,449 --> 00:15:03,449
Nonsense.

226
00:15:04,295 --> 00:15:05,929
And is it that makes you so miserable?

227
00:15:06,289 --> 00:15:07,530
Are you afraid now in daylight?

228
00:15:08,929 --> 00:15:13,750
No, but night will come again before long, and besides, I am unhappy.

229
00:15:14,579 --> 00:15:16,590
Very unhappy, for other things.

230
00:15:17,610 --> 00:15:18,610
What other things?

231
00:15:18,769 --> 00:15:19,929
Can you tell me some of them?

232
00:15:21,590 --> 00:15:24,769
How much I wished to reply fully to this question?

233
00:15:25,929 --> 00:15:28,789
How difficult it was to frame any answer.

234
00:15:29,670 --> 00:15:33,570
Children can feel, but they cannot analyze their feelings.

235
00:15:34,730 --> 00:15:38,677
And if the analysis is partially affected in thought, they

236
00:15:38,679 --> 00:15:42,030
know not how to express the result of the process in words.

237
00:15:43,219 --> 00:15:46,108
Fearful, however, of losing this first and only

238
00:15:46,110 --> 00:15:49,431
opportunity of relieving my grief by imparting it.

239
00:15:49,869 --> 00:15:56,510
I, after a disturbed pause, contrived to frame Amiga, though, as far as it went,

240
00:15:57,170 --> 00:15:58,170
true response.

241
00:15:59,269 --> 00:16:02,590
For one thing, I have no father or mother, brothers or sisters.

242
00:16:03,699 --> 00:16:05,289
You have a kind aunt and cousins.

243
00:16:06,460 --> 00:16:07,530
Again, I paused.

244
00:16:08,170 --> 00:16:09,909
Then bunglingly announced.

245
00:16:11,304 --> 00:16:14,829
But John read knocked me down, and my aunt shut me up in the red room.

246
00:16:16,610 --> 00:16:19,750
Mr. Lloyd, a second time, produced his snuff box.

247
00:16:21,610 --> 00:16:25,409
Don't you think Gateshead Hall, a very beautiful house, asked he?

248
00:16:26,590 --> 00:16:30,409
Are you not very thankful to have such a fine place to live at?

249
00:16:32,139 --> 00:16:33,349
It is not my house, sir.

250
00:16:34,349 --> 00:16:37,230
An abbot says, I have less right to be here than a servant.

251
00:16:38,369 --> 00:16:39,369
Poo.

252
00:16:39,739 --> 00:16:42,909
You can't be silly enough to wish to leave such a splendid place.

253
00:16:43,985 --> 00:16:48,119
If I had anywhere else to go, I should be glad to leave it,

254
00:16:48,119 --> 00:16:51,369
but I can never get away from Gateshead till I am a woman.

255
00:16:53,485 --> 00:16:55,670
Perhaps you may... who knows?

256
00:16:56,690 --> 00:16:58,449
Have you any relations besides Mrs. Reed?

257
00:16:59,519 --> 00:17:00,519
I think not, sir.

258
00:17:01,610 --> 00:17:03,530
None belonging to your father.

259
00:17:04,250 --> 00:17:05,250
I don't know.

260
00:17:05,570 --> 00:17:09,829
I asked Aunt Reed once, and she said possibly I might have some poor,

261
00:17:10,179 --> 00:17:14,030
low relations called air, but she knew nothing about them.

262
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:18,389
If you had such, would you like to go to them?

263
00:17:19,190 --> 00:17:20,190
I reflected.

264
00:17:21,090 --> 00:17:25,190
Poverty looks grim to grown people, still more so to children.

265
00:17:26,210 --> 00:17:30,449
They have not much idea of industrious working respectable poverty.

266
00:17:31,690 --> 00:17:35,489
They think of the word only as connected with ragged clothes, scanty food,

267
00:17:36,110 --> 00:17:39,769
fireless grates, rude manners and debasing vices.

268
00:17:40,530 --> 00:17:43,469
Poverty for me was synonymous with degradation.

269
00:17:44,809 --> 00:17:49,329
No, I should not like to belong to poor people, was my reply.

270
00:17:50,289 --> 00:17:52,409
Not even if they were kind to you.

271
00:17:53,630 --> 00:17:54,630
I shook my head.

272
00:17:55,755 --> 00:18:01,170
I could not see how poor people had the means of being kind, and then to learn to

273
00:18:01,170 --> 00:18:07,150
speak like them, to adopt their manners, to be uneducated, to grow up like one of

274
00:18:07,150 --> 00:18:10,949
the poor women I saw sometimes, nursing their children or washing their

275
00:18:10,951 --> 00:18:13,369
clothes at the cottage doors of the village of Gateshead.

276
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:21,030
No, I was not heroic enough to purchase liberty at the price of caste.

277
00:18:23,719 --> 00:18:25,690
But are your relatives so very poor?

278
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:27,670
Are they working people?

279
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:30,380
I cannot tell.

280
00:18:31,510 --> 00:18:34,869
Aunt Reed says if I have any, they must be a beggly set.

281
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:37,250
I should not like to go a begging.

282
00:18:39,269 --> 00:18:40,510
Would you like to go to school?

283
00:18:42,014 --> 00:18:43,610
Again, I reflected.

284
00:18:44,590 --> 00:18:46,489
I scarcely knew what school was.

285
00:18:47,719 --> 00:18:52,349
Bessie sometimes spoke of it as a place where young ladies sat in the stocks,

286
00:18:53,070 --> 00:18:57,969
war backboards, and were expected to be exceedingly gentile and precise.

287
00:18:58,929 --> 00:19:02,010
John Reed hated his school and abused his master.

288
00:19:03,190 --> 00:19:06,349
But John Reed's tastes were no rule for mine.

289
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:11,730
And if Bessie's accounts of school discipline gathered from the young ladies

290
00:19:11,731 --> 00:19:17,170
of a family where she had lived before coming to Gateshead were somewhat appalling.

291
00:19:18,630 --> 00:19:23,449
Her details of certain accomplishments attained by these same young ladies were,

292
00:19:24,094 --> 00:19:26,289
I thought, equally attractive.

293
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:32,230
She boasted of beautiful paintings of landscapes and flowers by them executed,

294
00:19:33,150 --> 00:19:37,099
of songs they could sing and pieces they could play, of

295
00:19:37,101 --> 00:19:40,150
purses they could of French books they could translate.

296
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:44,929
Till my spirit was moved to emulation as I listened.

297
00:19:45,650 --> 00:19:48,670
Besides, school would be a complete change.

298
00:19:49,170 --> 00:19:52,161
It implied a long journey, an entire separation

299
00:19:52,163 --> 00:19:55,110
from Gateshead, an entrance into a new life.

300
00:20:00,909 --> 00:20:08,909
I should indeed like to go to school was the as he got up.

301
00:20:09,849 --> 00:20:12,610
The child ought to have change of air and scene.

302
00:20:13,619 --> 00:20:17,969
He added, speaking to himself, nerves not in a good state.

303
00:20:19,429 --> 00:20:20,449
Bessie now returned.

304
00:20:21,230 --> 00:20:25,309
At the same moment, the carriage was heard rolling up the gravel walk.

305
00:20:26,650 --> 00:20:27,771
Is that your mistress nurse?

306
00:20:28,369 --> 00:20:29,369
asked Mr. Lloyd.

307
00:20:30,320 --> 00:20:32,449
I should like to speak to her before I go.

308
00:20:34,269 --> 00:20:39,530
Bessie invited him to walk into the breakfast room and led the way out.

309
00:20:40,929 --> 00:20:46,070
In the interview which followed between him and Mrs. Reed, I presume, from after

310
00:20:46,070 --> 00:20:51,690
occurrences, that the apothecary ventured to recommend my being sent to school,

311
00:20:52,719 --> 00:20:56,909
and the recommendation was no doubt readily enough adopted.

312
00:20:57,670 --> 00:21:02,829
For as Abbott said, in discussing the subject with Bessie, when both sat sowing

313
00:21:02,831 --> 00:21:08,789
in the nursery one night, after I was in bed, and, as they thought, a sleep.

314
00:21:09,869 --> 00:21:15,510
Mrs. Walsh, she did say glad enough to get rid of such a tiresome ill-conditioned

315
00:21:15,510 --> 00:21:20,829
child, who always looked as if she were watching everybody, and scheming plots

316
00:21:20,929 --> 00:21:21,949
underhand.

317
00:21:23,340 --> 00:21:28,929
Abbott, I think, gave me credit for being a sort of infantine guy forks.

318
00:21:30,010 --> 00:21:35,269
On that same occasion I learned, for the first time, from Mrs. Abbott s

319
00:21:35,271 --> 00:21:41,110
communications to Bessie, that my father had been a poor clergyman, that my mother

320
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:46,210
had married him against the wishes of her friends, who considered the match beneath

321
00:21:46,210 --> 00:21:52,550
her, that my grandfather Reed was so irritated at her disobedience he cut her

322
00:21:52,550 --> 00:21:57,369
off without a shilling, that after my mother and father had been married a year,

323
00:21:58,110 --> 00:22:02,650
the latter caught the typhus fever while visiting among the poor of a large

324
00:22:02,650 --> 00:22:08,889
manufacturing town, where his curacy was situated, and where that disease was then

325
00:22:08,891 --> 00:22:13,440
prevalent, that my mother took the infection from

326
00:22:13,442 --> 00:22:16,630
him, and both died within a month of each other.

327
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:21,152
Bessie, when she heard this narrative, sighed and

328
00:22:21,152 --> 00:22:24,429
said, poor Miss Jane is to be pitied to Abbott?

329
00:22:25,710 --> 00:22:27,690
Yes, responded Abbott.

330
00:22:28,690 --> 00:22:33,590
If she were a nice, pretty child, one might compassionate her for lawnness,

331
00:22:34,349 --> 00:22:37,730
but one really cannot care for such a little toad as that.

332
00:22:39,389 --> 00:22:42,469
Not a great deal to be sure, agreed Bessie.

333
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:49,471
At any rate, a beauty like Miss Georgiana would be more moving in the same condition.

334
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:54,150
Yes, I do it on Miss Georgiana, cried the fervent Abbott.

335
00:22:55,170 --> 00:22:59,471
Little darling, with her long curls and her blue eyes, and

336
00:22:59,472 --> 00:23:03,389
such a sweet color as she has, just as if she were painted.

337
00:23:04,300 --> 00:23:07,190
Bessie, I could fancy a Welsh rabbit for supper.

338
00:23:08,329 --> 00:23:10,309
So good eye, with a roast onion.

339
00:23:10,829 --> 00:23:11,829
Come, we'll go down.

340
00:23:12,809 --> 00:23:13,809
They went.
